---
title: "The Sippability Factor: Why beverages are always either hot, cold or fizzy."
description: "Does humour and absurdity have any effect on advertorial persuasion?"
date: 2026-05-20
draft: true
---

How can good drinks taste like hotdog water when warm, while mediocre drinks can taste amazing when ice cold? 

Why is a sip of a well designed drink more satiating than a hearty chug of a bad drink? 

I can think of five factors that influence sippability, or the ROI of a sip - temperature, carbonation, acidity, alcohol content and aroma. These factors are not really mutually exclusive - carbonation can effect acidity levels, temperature can effect carbonation levels, and so on. 

There are a number of other factors that can influence sippability to lesser degrees, like astringency which we will not discuss here.
